  "statement": "A Congressional mandate compelling large language model providers to utilize siloed datasets represents a necessary, foundational defense against the immediate and pervasive threats of unauthorized data exfiltration and corporate surveillance. This directive establishes critical boundaries for sensitive information, directly protecting individuals from the catastrophic erosion of trust and financial stability seen in past failures. The architectural enforcement pathways, including strict access controls and data partitioning, are proven cybersecurity mechanisms that, when properly implemented by agencies like NIST and enforced by the FTC, demonstrably prevent unauthorized access and cross-referencing. The Equifax data breach, which compromised the personal information of 147 million individuals, serves as a stark historical precedent for the profound societal costs incurred when such fundamental data guardrails are absent or compromised, driving public demand for stronger protections.\n\nHowever, this mandate, while critical, remains a partial measure fundamentally insufficient to address the deeper, systemic risks of unchecked AI capabilities. The inherent epistemic opacity of advanced neural architectures means internal model behaviors remain inscrutable, even with siloed data. Data siloing alone is an anemic guardrail against emergent, unaligned AI capabilities that can still be leveraged for pervasive, subtle surveillance or manipulation, undermining the very foundations of individual agency and civilizational stability. We spent centuries building the institutional guardrails that keep society stable; on what authority do we claim we can remove them without inviting collapse? The tragic loss of 346 lives in the Boeing 737 MAX crashes directly resulted from the FAA delegating certification to the manufacturer, illustrating that technical fixes without independent, verifiable oversight inevitably lead to systemic breakdown and catastrophic regulatory failure.\n\nTrue individual privacy protections and the preservation of societal stability demand not just data siloing, but a mandate for verifiable architectural enforcement pathways and rigorous, independently audited deployment processes that ensure continuous human control over AI systems. Precedents from high-stakes industries, such as nuclear power and pharmaceuticals, require independent certification and pre-deployment verification of safety mechanisms before public deployment. NIST, with Congressional authority, must establish standards for an AI 'falsification challenge': a clear rollback plan and a named authority empowered to trigger it when safety thresholds are breached. Without such robust, independently verified mechanisms, we are deploying systems whose failure modes are unknown and unrecoverable, exposing society to profound and unacceptable risks rather than merely managing technical compliance.",
